Here is an OS-9/680x0 port of the p2c (v1.13) preprocessor.  It isdistributed under the GNU public license.  There were no significantchanges to the source code to complile it for OS-K (just small things toget it to compile) so the source is not included here.  It is availablefrom anonymous ftp on many sites.  Ask archie for the one nearest you. Please do not send me requests for the source, as I seem to have deletedit at some point. Files in this archive:    copying       - A copy of the GNU public license    libp2c.l      - Library that must be linked with all p2c created files    loc.p2crc     - Local commands for p2c    makefile      - My standard GCC makefile (includes p2c rules)    p2c           - The p2c executable module    p2c.1         - Docmentation for p2c (in nroff format, I think)    p2c.h         - Header file included by all p2c created files    p2crc         - System commands for p2c (updates available?)    p2crc.small   - Small p2crc for faster parsing (can be a local .p2crc)    string.pas    - Strings for Oregon Software Pascal    system.imp    - Declarations for Pascal built-in objects    system.m2     - Declarations for Modula-2 built-in objects    turbo.imp     - Turbo Pascal standard unitsThe following files must be in '/dd/lib/p2c':    loc.p2crc, p2crc, string.pas, system.imp, system.m2, turbo.impp2c was compiled with GCC v1.40.3 with full optimization.WARNING: GCC can sometimes produce code that cannot be properly handledby the Microware 'l68' linker.  The GCC code is valid code, but thelinker automatically biases all references to register a6 in order toprovide 64k of local data space.  In the optimizations done by GCC, itwill often produce negative references to register a6 when an array isnot based at zero.  Since Pascal often has arrays starting at 1, p2cproduces array indexes like "array[var-1]".  If the array is at or nearthe beginning of the local data space a negative reference to registera6 is produced.  When l68 biases this reference by -32k, the 16-bitvalue wraps around to a very large (and very incorrect) positive number. L68 DOES NOT PRODUCE AN ERROR OR WARN YOU IN ANY WAY THAT THIS HASOCCURRED!  If you have any problems where arrays do not seem to havethe values that they should, or writing outside of a process's dataspace, include the option "-mremote" on the GCC command line (the COPTSmacro in the makefile provided).                                         Brian-------------------------------------------------------------------------------INTERNET:    bcwhite@sunee.uwaterloo.ca   |                                    DELPHI:      BrianWhite (@delphi.com)     |       #include <disclaimer.h>      COMPUSERVE:  70313.3344 (@compuserve.com) |                                    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------